From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 13:10:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: a middleware is told which gateway key called, by id
 and name (#5463, marrypoter2023)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A gateway key's owner asks the key itself to choose its routing group:
key A to group/fast, key B to group/smart, the client's config
untouched. A middleware can rewrite body.model to group/<g> before
routing (middleware-model-map's shape), but neither it nor the plugins'
gateway hooks could tell which key asked: gwmw.Info carried only
protocol, model, stream, path and agent, and GatewayContext the same
plus origin and session — the docs pass no Authorization on, and the
key's id never left the request's context. Both now carry Key and
KeyName (JSON key, keyName): the calling gateway key's id and its
current name, never the secret, absent when there is none, the same
id and name the trace and the routing history already keep
(5fe4c48df). With that, a community middleware maps key ids to
routing groups exactly as the issue sketches; keys with no mapping,
keyless requests, groups, strategies, rules, limits and the per-key
models and accounts restrictions all go as they did.

TestMiddlewareSeesCallerKey drives a key request and a keyless one
through a JS middleware that appends ctx.key and ctx.keyName to the
ask, and the keyed one through the provider with the secret never
shown; TestGatewayHooksSeeCallerKey does the same for a Bun hook's
onRequest stash and onResponse report. Both fail on the old code and
with either fill ablated; they pass filled. Run: the two tests
-count=1 and -race ok; ./internal/gateway -tags nogui ok;
./internal/gwmw ./internal/middleware ok, ./internal/plugin ok but
TestHostToldTheGatewayAddress, red at the parent eef99e4bbe too;
gofmt and vet clean; builds on darwin/arm64 and windows/amd64 ok, the
linux tree red at wails v3.0.0-beta.24 as before, my four packages
build there.
